Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a refund is requested for a reason other than "damaged" (including a blank reason or any other reason, such as "just because"), and the item is not marked final sale, the refund is the full amount if requested up to day 14 after delivery, half the amount if requested from day 15 to day 30, and nothing if requested after day 30. This applies to every such item, including custom-made and perishable ones. For example, Meera's Rs 2,000 jacket at day 45 gets no refund, and Rs 4,000 unworn shoes returned 5 days after delivery for a changed mind get the full Rs 4,000.
  2. Whenever a refund is requested with the reason "damaged", and it is requested up to 90 days after delivery, the refund is the full amount plus the delivery (shipping) fee. For example, a Rs 1,000 kurta with Rs 100 delivery refunds Rs 1,100. Whenever it is requested after day 90, nothing is refunded. This also applies to final-sale items, and the normal 14-day and 30-day schedule does not apply to damaged goods.
  3. Whenever a returned item is marked final sale and did not arrive damaged, no refund is given.
  4. Whenever a refund amount is worked out, it starts from the sum of item price times quantity for the items being returned, minus the discount code. For example, a full return of Neha's Rs 5,000 of items with a Rs 500 coupon is worked out on Rs 4,500. The delivery fee is never refunded on a return, except when the goods arrived damaged. A changed-mind return therefore refunds only the item price, or half of it after day 14.
  5. Whenever the refund would come to under Rs 50, nothing is paid, unless the goods arrived damaged.
  6. Whenever a refund is paid, it goes back to the payment method the order was originally paid with, whatever the reason for the refund. Orders paid with store credit are refunded as store credit, and orders paid by card are refunded to that card. It is not always store credit.
  7. Whenever a customer returns unworn shoes within 14 days of delivery because they changed their mind, and paid by card or credit, no handling fee is charged and the full purchase price is refunded.
  8. Whenever a refund is requested for an order that has already been fully refunded, no additional money is refunded.
  9. Whenever a refund is requested for an order that was cancelled before delivery, no refund is issued automatically. The request is held for the owner to review personally before any money moves.
  10. Whenever an order is placed, it earns 1 loyalty point for every full Rs 100 of the item total after the discount code is applied, rounded down (Rs 1,250 earns 12 points). This applies only if the order was not paid with store credit and the item total after the discount code is at least Rs 500 (exactly Rs 500 does earn points). Otherwise it earns no points. Final-sale orders earn double points.
  11. Whenever an order is refunded, in full or in part, the system must not automatically change, deduct, keep or prorate loyalty points. What happens to the points on a refund is undefined until the owner decides (for example, Rohit's 20 points after a Rs 1,000 partial refund of his Rs 2,000 order).
  12. Whenever an order is partly paid with loyalty points, the number of points it earns is undefined, and the owner will decide it himself (for example, Pooja's Rs 1,000 order paid with Rs 600 of points and Rs 400 by card). The policy does not cover loyalty points used as payment.
  13. Whenever the daily sales report is produced, "Orders" is the count of every order in the list given, including store-credit orders.
  14. Whenever the daily sales report is produced, "Revenue" is the sum, over card orders only, of each order's total, where an order's total is its items after discount plus shipping. Store-credit orders are not included. Refunds are not subtracted (with Rs 10,000 of sales and a Rs 1,500 refund today, the figure is not reduced by the refund).
  15. Whenever the daily sales report shows the average order value, it is revenue divided by the number of card orders (not all orders), rounded down to a whole rupee, and 0 if there are no card orders. For example, Rs 3,001 revenue across 3 card orders displays as Rs 1,000.
  16. Whenever the daily sales report is produced for a day with no orders at all, it shows 0 orders, Rs 0 revenue and Rs 0 average.
  17. Whenever the daily sales report is produced, it uses whatever list of orders it is given, without applying any built-in rule that assigns an order to a particular day (for example, by order time or by payment time). The owner will decide the day question himself.
  18. Whenever the daily sales report meets cancelled orders, unpaid (cash on delivery) orders or fully refunded orders, how they are treated is undefined, and the owner will decide.
